## Why loading secrets from a plain‑text `.env` file may be insufficient

| Concern | Explanation |
|---------|-------------|
| **Persistence at rest** | A `.env` file lives on disk in clear text. If the file is ever committed to a repository (or copied to a backup), the secret is exposed. |
| **Rotation & audit** | Updating the secret requires editing the file on every host, which is error‑prone and makes it difficult to audit who changed it and when. |
| **Least‑privilege access** | All services that run the container get the entire file, even if they only need a subset of the variables. |
| **Compliance** | Many standards (PCI‑DSS, SOC2, ISO‑27001) require secrets to be stored in a managed vault with access controls and audit logging. |
| **Platform portability** | Cloud‑native platforms (EKS, ECS, Cloud Run, Azure App Service, etc.) expect you to inject secrets via their own secret‑management APIs rather than mounting a raw file. |

